A||||4||Quadfiero||88 Koni valving||||1||
Z||000000||Quadfiero||03-15-2001||03:55 PM||||I just got my Konis for my '88 and found out that the fronts are the same part number as the Chevelle/Malibu 78-85. Does this seem strange to anyone else? I would guess the front of the Fiero weighs about half as much as a Malibu. Does anyone have experience with the Konis on an "88?||||reg||1
Z||000001||gixxer||03-15-2001||07:21 PM||||Saw that also. They seem to work well anyway.<BR>Mine are set at full soft. <BR>Considering the stiff lowering springs, poly bushes and 50 series tires, my ride quality is quite good. <BR>The front end sticks very well in corners also.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||03-15-2001||08:57 PM||||Mine had the konis on it when I bought it 2 years ago. They were set about halfway when I got it and weren't much fun on potholes. I set them full soft and they ride good like that. I started autocrossing a little last summer and they seemed to do good at that too. Being a novice, I didn't exactly push it to the limit though. I also remember seeing a couple of autocross guys somewhere in cyberspace recommending leaving them full soft because the front end is so light. I might play with them a little this year just to see what happens.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||03-16-2001||12:46 AM||||The weight of the car won't matter as much,it's the relative stiffness(motion frequency)that'll determine compatability.<BR>My front Bilsteins are for a Toyota pickup,cost $50 each.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Quadfiero||03-16-2001||12:58 PM||||Thanks guys,<BR>I was worried about the ride more than anything. I apreciate the information.<BR>Bob<BR>||||reg||1
